  "query_preview": "..\n\nOccluding low pressure continues to lift into the northern Great \nLakes shifting LLJ structure over the Mississippi up into the \nsouthern Great Lakes. Connection to the Gulf draws anomalous \nmoisture into SE MI this morning as PW values push 1.5\", near daily \nclimatological maxima, supporting widespread showers throughout the \nmorning into this afternoon. Gusty southerly winds also accompany \nthis structure as diurnal mixing at least partially taps into 30-\n35kt flow at 925mb resulting in wind gusts generally in the 25-30mph \nthough with peak potential towards 35mph. LLJ forcing shifts east \ninto Ontario by the latter half of the afternoon tapering off wider \nspread rain and transitioning to pockets of lingering drizzle/light \nrain. Secondary window for greater coverage arrives this evening as \na broken line of showers tied to the cold front cross the state. \n\nSignificantly drier airmass trails this cold fropa late tonight as \nthe system's dry slot works across SE MI through the first half of \nThursday. Southwesterly winds post-frontal winds moderate the \nmagnitude of cold advection keeping slightly above normal \ntemperatures around with highs in the upper 50s to around 60. The, \nby this point, closed low then sinks southeast across Lake Huron \nlatter half of Thursday into Friday offering another period of \nscattered light showers. Secondary cold front eventually sweeps \nthrough the region Friday morning bringing both a window of enhanced \nshower coverage as well as briefly ushering in a seasonably average \nairmass to close out the week. Near to sub 0C 850mb temps result in \nhighs only around 50, give or take a couple degrees either side, \nboth Friday and Saturday. Long range models favoring low amplitude \nridging redeveloping over the central CONUS by early next week which \nwould bring back above normal temps to SE MI.",
